WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.120
When you watch a major motion picture, you'd
never imagine that a huge production would

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.339
make any last-minute decisions.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:10.460
Or if they did, it would surely be over something
small, not something that could drastically

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.690
change the movie forever.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.790
But really, the opposite is true.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.550
Some of your favorite films were within in
an inch of turning out terrible, if it wasn't

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.740
for a last-second change that made these movies
into what they are today.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.179
Toy Story

00:00:23.179 --> 00:00:28.109
Before Toy Story became a classic of computer
animation, it was almost a mean-spirited cartoon

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:30.710
about a jerky cowboy doll that everyone hated.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:35.170
Jeffrey Katzenberg, Disney's CEO at the time,
had wanted this cartoon in a new medium to

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.730
be as edgy as possible.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.399
Eager to please, the Pixar folks pushed the
meanness to the max and hoped it was going

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:41.750
in the right direction.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.200
When they screened the first part of the film,
no one could believe the results.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.370
It was just a story about mean, hateful toys.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.680
There was no fun and nothing lovable.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.870
In this version, Woody intentionally tosses
Buzz out the window.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.899
Then, the toys revolt against him and throw
him out the window to his own doom.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:00.541
Even director John Lasseter couldn't believe
how bad it was.

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:05.150
"I was pretty much embarrassed with what was
on the screen."

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:09.270
The result: the execs wanted Lasseter to fire
a bunch of people and start animating under

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.730
Disney's direct supervision.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.090
He refused, and asked for two weeks to make
it work.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.050
Disney allowed it and, after working all hours
and disregarding all the previous notes, the

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.100
Pixar team was able to present enough of the
revised Toy Story to keep their jobs and keep

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.270
going with the film.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.470
In the end, the story was completely retooled
and became the wonderful movie we all love

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:28.510
today.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.960
Zootopia

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.800
In its original form, Zootopia hardly resembled
the film we know now.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.830
First of all, Judy Hopps wasn't the main character.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.490
Plus, all the predators had to wear shock
collars around their necks for their whole

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:40.490
lives.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.640
If that sounds unpleasant, don't worry, the
Zootopia filmmakers agreed.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.720
In the original script, once predators got
to a certain age, they'd have to put on their

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.920
taming collars for the first time.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.960
Watching this happy little bear realize he'll
never be free again is heartbreaking.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.920
Though Disney doesn't usually shy away from
heartbreak, the filmmakers eventually felt

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.040
the overall taming concept made the movie
too dark and unappealing.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.510
So, they revamped the entire script, made
Judy the main character, and threw those collars

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.410
in the idea-garbage.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.899
American Beauty

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:12.920
Before winning five Academy Awards, American
Beauty nearly had a different ending that

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.180
may have kept it out if the Oscars altogether.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.290
In the first draft of the script, the story
is bookended by a court trial in which Lester's

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.060
daughter Jane and her boyfriend, Ricky, are
accused of shooting Lester.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.760
The trial uses the tape they made where Jane
says she'd like Ricky to get rid of her dad

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.760
as the proof.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.510
The film ends with Ricky and Jane getting
wrongfully convicted and sent to jail.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.560
In the end, a last minute edit came to the
rescue.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:40.400
"We shot all of that stuff, but, uh, luckily
Sam, you know in editing, sorta took it out."

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.090
Screenwriter Alan Ball agreed with the change,
feeling that the ending was too depressing.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.750
That might sound strange for a film that already
ends with the main character getting shot,

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.790
but the innocent teens taking the blame was
a step too far.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.880
Plus, it took away from Lester's main story,
so the wraparound was cut and Ricky and Jane

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.630
got to go free.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:58.840
Shrek

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.380
The character of Shrek went through a lot
of changes before we ended up with the Scottish

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.380
ogre with a heart-of-gold.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.290
Originally, Mike Myers recorded most of his
lines in a Canadian accent.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.440
It was basically, Myers' own voice with a
bit of exaggeration.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.410
But the actor felt it wasn't working right.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.961
Inspired by the upper-class English accent
John Lithgow used for Lord Farquaad, Myers

00:03:16.961 --> 00:03:19.530
felt Shrek should have a more working-class
sound.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.620
He ended up settling on Scottish and insisted
on redoing all the lines with his new idea.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.630
"Look, there's Bloodnut the Flatulent.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.740
You can guess what he's famous for."

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.010
"Alright, now I KNOW you're making this up."

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:36.190
Unfortunately, the animators had already finished
a third of film, and the new voice required

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:37.260
a ton of changes.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:43.160
In fact, Myers' choice to go Scottish cost
the film at least $4 million.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.200
Clerks

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.700
Kevin Smith made a big splash with his independent
film debut, Clerks.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.550
A black and white comedy about two aimless
men working at a convenience store, Clerks

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.490
set up characters that Smith would return
to for years to come.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:59.340
Part of what makes Clerks great was its casual,
comedic dialogue and slice-of-life tone.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.970
That all could have been ruined by the original
ending.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.340
Originally, Dante, one of the Quick Stop workers,
gets shot in a robbery and the film ends right

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.160
after that.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:18.200
Brian O'Halloran, the actor who played Dante
said, "I hated that ending.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:20.130
I just thought it was too quick of a twist."

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.100
As it turned out, Smith wasn't married to
that finale either.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.100
He shot it mostly because he wasn't sure what
to do to end the movie.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.500
Luckily, he agreed with O'Halloran and went
with the non-tragic ending, where the simply

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.000
store closes for the evening, and life goes
on.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.240
Terminator 2

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.880
The ending of Terminator 2 is, literally,
the most metal ending of all time: a robot

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.240
gets lowered into a vat of molten metal.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.990
So imagine if, after that, the movie cuts
to a bright blue sky over a playground where

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.820
granny Sarah Connor is tying her cute little
grandchild's shoes.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.070
That's originally how director James Cameron
wanted it to end.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.570
In that ending, Judgment Day never happened,
John Connor is a senator, and Sarah is an

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.300
old lady with a drinking problem.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.920
Thankfully, Cameron realized that this ending
was completely out of tone with the rest of

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.639
the film and would have ruined any chances
for sequels.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:10.360
Or...maybe he finally saw the wisdom in the
Terminator's own words.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.270
"It has to end here."

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:12.920
Nah.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.480
It was totally about the sequels.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.820
Thanks for watching!

